    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_f468b948f0356e11591db8922ffde74c458d3a950287f6aef49cc440d461b43b",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source treats kernel shapes and positive cosine-polynomial coefficients as variable inputs to a common constrained proof interface: a candidate must support the explicit-formula setup and endpoint or transform conditions, then pass correction-negativity and kernel-monotonicity checks before its resulting R0 can be compared. The autocorrelation construction moves part of that burden into the representation itself by producing a kernel stated to satisfy conditions (1) and (4), while the final remarks use equal rounded output and one worse alternative as limited sensitivity checks rather than evidence of a unique or global optimum.",
